(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with an inferior myocardial infarction. His heart rate falls to 46 with narrow QRS complexes and P waves that are either inverted (retrograde P waves) or buried in the QRS. Which rhythm is most likely?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia","Junctional rhythm","Sinus bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow rhythm at 40 to 60 with inverted or buried P waves is a junctional rhythm, and inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with decompensated heart failure and chronic lung disease develops an irregular tachycardia with three different P-wave shapes in one lead. What does this rhythm signal about her course?","choices":["A pacemaker is needed","A poor prognosis","A benign finding","Imminent recovery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Multifocal atrial tachycardia is a poor prognostic sign, occurring in acutely ill patients with severe lung disease or heart failure and right heart stress. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a slow sinus rhythm at 34 is hypotensive and drowsy. Two doses of atropine have not changed his rate or blood pressure. Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral metoprolol","Temporary pacemaker","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When atropine fails in acute, symptomatic, unstable bradycardia, the next step is a temporary pacemaker; a permanent pacemaker follows if the cause will not resolve. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 210. His blood pressure is 72/40, and he is confused and sweating. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Unsynchronized defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia (hypotension and confusion) gets immediate synchronized cardioversion, a timed shock at decreased voltage. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions despite cutting out caffeine and alcohol. She has no contraindications to beta blockade. Which treatment is first?","choices":["Warfarin","Metoprolol","Procainamide","Adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has new pauses and slow rates on an ambulatory monitor. He started verapamil and digoxin last month for palpitations. He feels mildly lightheaded but is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Atropine tablets","Stop the rate-slowing drugs","Permanent pacemaker now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medications (beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin) are listed causes of sick sinus syndrome, and management starts by treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has a delta wave (a slurred upstroke of the QRS) with a short PR interval on a screening electrocardiogram but no symptoms. His parents ask what the finding means. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["A slow node needing a pacemaker","An extra pathway causing fast rhythms","A sign of a prior heart attack","A normal athletic heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pre-excitation with a delta wave means an accessory pathway (the bundle of Kent), which carries the potential for reentrant tachycardia, as in Wolff-Parkinson-White. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable atrial fibrillation at 92 asks why the plan is to control his heart rate rather than shock him back into normal rhythm today. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["His rhythm can never be restored","Slowing the rate dissolves clots","Rate control is the first goal","Shocks are never used for it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In hemodynamically stable atrial fibrillation the first goal is rate control, with anticoagulation; elective cardioversion can follow after 21 days of anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman is brought in after a car crash with a heart rate of 128 and a blood pressure of 88/56. The rhythm is regular with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Bedside ultrasound shows free fluid in the abdomen. Which is the most appropriate approach to her heart rate?","choices":["Intravenous metoprolol","Treat the blood loss","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is appropriate sinus tachycardia from hypovolemia, a pathologic cause; management of appropriate sinus tachycardia is treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 180 is being treated in the emergency department. She is alert with a normal blood pressure. Which treatment should be avoided at this stage?","choices":["Unsynchronized defibrillation","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","A second adenosine dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unsynchronized defibrillation is for pulseless rhythms; a stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine, and only an unstable one with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has frequent, disruptive episodes of atrioventricular nodal reentry and wants a definitive cure. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Valve replacement","Septal myectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation offers a select subset of patients with nodal reentry the opportunity for a definitive cure, in over 95%. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is told his electrocardiogram shows a normal P wave before each narrow QRS at 68 beats per minute, with the rate rising as he breathes in and falling as he breathes out. He worries that he has heart disease. Which counseling is most accurate?","choices":["A normal variant; no treatment","He needs a pacemaker workup","He should stop exercising","He needs an anticoagulant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sinus rate that speeds with inspiration and slows with expiration, with normal P waves and a narrow QRS, is sinus arrhythmia, a normal physiologic variant that needs no treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man has a junctional rhythm at 42 with dizziness. Monitoring shows that the cause is high-grade atrioventricular block. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Observation","Adenosine","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm due to complete or high-grade atrioventricular block or sick sinus syndrome is treated with a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has abrupt-onset palpitations during a week of little sleep and heavy coffee. Her heart rate is 160 and regular, and every QRS is preceded by an identical P wave whose shape differs from her baseline P wave. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia with one consistent, abnormally shaped P wave before each QRS, starting abruptly in someone without heart disease under increased metabolic demand, is focal atrial tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has frequent premature atrial contractions on a monitor (early beats with abnormal P waves), many per minute. He has no known heart disease. Which evaluation is most appropriate?","choices":["Test for an accessory pathway","Serum digoxin level","No evaluation is needed","Look for structural heart disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If premature atrial contractions are frequent, look for underlying structural heart disease; in a diseased heart they may precede supraventricular tachycardia, fibrillation or flutter. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with symptomatic sinus bradycardia at 45 also has hypertension that needs a new medication. Which antihypertensive should be avoided?","choices":["Losartan","Verapamil","Lisinopril","Chlorthalidone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil is a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, and these drugs, like beta blockers and digoxin, cause sinus bradycardia; it would slow her further. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation at 150 has crackles throughout both lungs from pulmonary edema and a blood pressure of 82/50. Which feature makes this presentation a medical emergency?","choices":["Her age over 65","Pulmonary edema with hypotension","A heart rate above 100","An irregularly irregular pulse"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medical emergencies from atrial fibrillation include severe heart failure (pulmonary edema, hypotension) and atrial fibrillation with an acute myocardial infarction. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has sinus pauses of up to 2 seconds at night on an ambulatory monitor. Her occasional dizziness happens only when she stands up quickly and never during the pauses. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Catheter ablation","Observation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In sick sinus syndrome, management confirms whether symptoms correlate with the bradycardia; when they do not, observation is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has known tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) and asks which fast rhythm is behind his palpitations. An ambulatory monitor is arranged. Which rhythm is it most likely to record during the fast episodes?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Junctional rhythm","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome the tachyarrhythmia that alternates with bradycardia or pauses is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) on monitoring, and he faints during the pauses. Thyroid tests and electrolytes are normal and he takes no rate-slowing drugs. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Atropine as needed","Catheter ablation alone","Observation alone","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When symptoms correlate with bradycardia in sick sinus syndrome a permanent pacemaker is placed, and tachy-brady syndrome often needs one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man started a new blood pressure medication two weeks ago. He now feels tired, and his pulse is 46 and regular, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Thyroid tests are normal. Which medication is the most likely cause?","choices":["Metoprolol","Amlodipine","Lisinopril","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers such as metoprolol, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin are the drug causes of sinus bradycardia; a new one two weeks ago is the likely culprit. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a slow sinus rhythm at 38 six hours after an inferior myocardial infarction. She feels faint and her blood pressure is 80/50. Each narrow QRS has a normal P wave before it. Which drug should be given first?","choices":["Diltiazem","Adenosine","Atropine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia, here from an infarction affecting the conduction tissue, is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has frequent premature junctional contractions, more than 10 per minute, on a monitor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Check for heart disease","Reassurance only","Permanent pacemaker","Anticoagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Frequent premature junctional contractions (more than 10 per minute, or multifocal) prompt a search for structural heart disease, with a beta blocker to treat them. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation started an antiarrhythmic and then develops atrial flutter. Suddenly her ventricular rate matches her atrial rate at 280, with hypotension. Which complication is she now at high risk of?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Complete heart block","Sinus arrest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. 1:1 atrial flutter, where every flutter wave conducts, causes extreme hemodynamic instability and carries a big risk of progression to ventricular fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man had coronary bypass surgery two months ago. He now has palpitations and fatigue. His heart rate is 150 and regular, and the electrocardiogram shows sawtooth flutter waves, two for every narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sawtooth flutter waves with two for every narrow QRS are atrial flutter with 2:1 block, the most common ratio, and atrial scarring after surgery is a classic setting. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with known atrioventricular nodal reentry has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 178. She is stable. Which treatment almost always terminates it?","choices":["Vagal maneuvers or adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentry almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is rarely required. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man whose Wolff-Parkinson-White was found on a school electrocardiogram presents with palpitations. The rhythm is irregular and wide-complex, at rates up to 260, with varying delta waves (slurred upstrokes). He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74. Which drug is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Atropine","Intravenous procainamide","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Irregular, wide, very fast complexes in a patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White are pre-excited atrial fibrillation, treated with intravenous procainamide; adenosine must never be used. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation at 146 with palpitations. She is alert, her blood pressure is 132/84, and she has no chest pain or breathlessness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion","Immediate ablation","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; rate control is the first goal. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with known Wolff-Parkinson-White has palpitations. Her rhythm is irregularly irregular at rates up to 280 with wide, varying QRS complexes. She is alert with a blood pressure of 110/70. Which treatment must be avoided?","choices":["Electrical cardioversion","Later pathway ablation","Adenosine","Procainamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, adenosine must never be used: blocking the normal atrioventricular node leaves the accessory pathway to conduct the fibrillation at rates approaching 300. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with atrial fibrillation takes warfarin because direct oral anticoagulants are not suitable for him. Which international normalized ratio is the target?","choices":["1 to 1.5","3.5 to 4.5","4 to 5","2 to 3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If warfarin is used for anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation, the target international normalized ratio is 2 to 3. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
